00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.670 align:start position:0%
 
we're talking about how the coronavirus  has dramatically affected college  admissions hundreds of schools including  harvard and yale  will not require s-a-t or a-c-t scores  this year and many families wonder if  that will lead to a permanent change  well the journalist jeff selingo got a  rare inside look  at the application review process he  embedded  in the admissions offices of emory  university  davidson college and the university of  washington this is a very rare thing  this is unbelievable access and his new  book is called  who gets in and why a year inside  college admissions  it's published by scribner an imprint of  simon schuster which is part of vicom  cbs  and jeff selingo joins us now first on  cbs this morning  jeff thank you for being here the first  thing i want to dispel for people out  there is the idea that applying to  college is some sort of meritocracy your  book makes clear it is not  no it was never fair and it never will  be fair we  teenagers seem to think that a rejection  from their dream college is an  indictment on their life  but really what it really is about is  all the  agendas that colleges have all their  priorities that they have  that come through in admissions so they  may want more full pace students  more students from the dakotas so they  have all these priorities that come  through in admissions  so just because a college has a 25  acceptance rate  doesn't mean you have a one in four  chance of getting in yeah i love the  detail  that the typical admissions officer  looks at an application for just about  seven minutes think of the hours and the  agony that go into the application and  then a seven minute review  um but how has the the process changed  from the time when parents might have  been applying to school to now their  kids applying to school  well their kids now are applying to so  many more schools than parents did  eight or ten and so what's now happening  is that the smartest kids from  buffalo and from washington and from  seattle are applying  to the same set of schools all across  the country and now you throw in kids  around the globe applying to those same  schools so a school like emory  university which is one of the schools  that i embedded myself in  just four years ago they had 20 000  applications the year i was there  last year 30 000 applications so a rise  of 10  000 applications just in four years wow  i know a lot of parents and a lot of  kids right now are worried about how the  pandemic will impact their application  they're not part of clubs they could  have been part of they're not  volunteering where they might have  volunteered  how will the pandemic affect the  application process  well the biggest thing i think is the  sat and the act so tomorrow  the sats was supposed to be given to 300  000  teenagers and half of them have already  been cancelled  so what we're seeing as you mentioned in  the introduction we have 400 plus  colleges  that have gone test optional this year  which means they're not using test  scores you don't have to submit them  but yet we still see students out there  seniors trying to get a test score  because in the ambiguity of admissions  we think that number  really matters and what i found in the  year i was embedded in these three  admissions offices is that high school  grades and high school courses matter a  lot more than that test score  yeah and and the grades matter  throughout the senior year so even if  you're in you've got to continue to do  well  because or even if you're you're on the  bubble you've got to continue to do well  because the schools  are still checking believe it or not uh  jeff  you know with this inside look into the  process i'm curious what would you  change how can these schools do a better  job  well one of the things i think they  should stop doing is trying to recruit  all these students who don't have a  chance  in the book i talk all about the direct  marketing and if you have a  teenager who's been going to college  they probably get overwhelmed with  mail on their mailbox and email and  those are from schools that are just  trying to increase  their application numbers they have no  desire in some cases to actually meet  you  so one of the things i would change is  to try to slow down on that and cut back  on that direct marketing because you're  you're increasing students anxiety and  you're encouraging them to apply when  they have no chance  boy jeff i wish your book would have  been around when i was applying because  you can just beat yourself up when you  don't get into the school that you want  so when you say embedded what exactly  were you doing were you watching all the  admissions officers  look at applications so you could see  their thought process i'm fascinated by  what you did  yeah it was totally open process i was  allowed to be there whenever i wanted to  be i could look at any application and i  was reading applications  with them the only thing i couldn't do  is identify them by name but i could  identify  all the other details which i have in  the book and so i would sit there and i  would read and listen to the  conversations they were having about  individual students i was there during  what they call committee  where they meet around a big table and  they're voting up or down  in just the couple of minutes it's  amazing whether to accept somebody or to  reject somebody  you've got to hope that they're in a  good mood that their tummies are full  and they've had a good night's sleep so  if we want to get into college what's  the best thing we need to do  what you can control and so focus on  your high school courses you're going to  take  and the grades don't worry about what  happens in that admissions office  because so much of that is out of your  control  do the best you can do participate in  activities and then let the chips fall  where they may  and and read this new book by jeff  solingo because honestly  the scenes in the book are amazing you  actually see admissions officers moving  kids from  admit to not admit and then back and  you're like oh my god oh man  it's amazing jeff congratulations and  thank you for joining us  and the book again is uh who gets in and  why it is on sale now you'll want to  pick it up

